design:
POST /api/posts/publish   body { itemLocalId, visibility, commentary }
POST /api/posts/unpublish body { itemLocalId }
PATCH /api/account/profile/sections body { showFollowers?, showFollowing?, showReposts?, showReplies?, showLikes?, likesVisibility? }

For publish, follow SupabaseCollectionPublicationStore.publish(): read the owner current archive snapshot, find the item by local id, derive, validate the derived value, then call the RPC. Throw a typed not-found error when the item is absent, matching CollectionNotFoundError.

For the sections PATCH, validate in a pure function (src/lib/profileSections.ts) that rejects unknown keys and rejects likesVisibility === "unlisted", mirroring the database check. Every field is optional; an empty body is a 400, not a no-op success.

profileSections.ts also exports the read-side helper the view layer needs:
  visibleSections(profile, viewer): { followers, following, reposts, replies, likes }
- for the owner every section is present, flagged hidden where switched off;
- for everyone else a switched-off section is absent entirely, count included.

  export type Visibility = "private" | "followers" | "unlisted" | "public";
  export type Viewer =
    | { kind: "anonymous" }
    | { kind: "signed-in"; id: string }
    | { kind: "follower"; id: string }
    | { kind: "owner"; id: string };
  export function canSee(visibility: Visibility, viewer: Viewer): boolean;

The truth table, stated so the test can assert it literally:
- private:   owner only.
- followers: owner and follower.
- unlisted:  every viewer kind CAN see it by direct link - but unlisted rows are excluded from profile listings and Discover at the QUERY level, not here. Document that distinction in the module comment; it is the single most likely place for a future contributor to get confused.
- public:    every viewer kind.

Also export a listing-oriented helper so the query-level exclusion is not re-derived at each call site:
  export function isListable(visibility: Visibility, viewer: Viewer): boolean;  // canSee AND not (unlisted and not owner)

Test in src/lib/visibility.test.mjs as an explicit sixteen-entry fixture table, written out in full rather than generated, so a future change to one cell is visible in the diff.

Skeleton:

  export const dynamic = "force-dynamic";
  const loadProfile = cache(async (handle: string) => { ... resolveHandle + listOwnedPublications ... });
  export async function generateMetadata({ params }) { ... }
  export default async function ProfilePage({ params, searchParams }) { ... }

Follow the comment already in /c/[slug]/page.tsx: a thrown error (a Supabase outage) must reach Next error boundary rather than being swallowed into notFound() - only a genuinely missing handle reads as 404.

AppShell takes section="account". No change to the AppSection union in PrimaryNav.tsx: "account" is already a member with no LINKS entry, so the nav renders with nothing highlighted.

Tabs are ?tab= search params rendered as links (searchParams is a Promise in this Next version - await it, as WelcomePage already does). Valid values this phase: overview (default) and collections; anything else falls back to overview rather than 404ing.

generateMetadata: title of the form "<displayName> (@<handle>) - Coeus", description from the bio. An OG image is explicitly out of scope for this task - file it separately if wanted.

Single transaction:
1. alter table public.collection_publications add column owner_id uuid references auth.users(id) on delete cascade;
2. update public.collection_publications p set owner_id = a.owner_id from public.archives a where a.id = p.archive_id;
3. alter table public.collection_publications alter column owner_id set not null;
4. create index collection_publications_owner_live_idx on public.collection_publications (owner_id, published_at desc) where unpublished_at is null;

Then set owner_id inside publish_collection() so it can never drift from archives.owner_id. The function already receives p_owner_id and already validates it against the archive owner (see the raise exception "Archive not found for owner" guard), so the insert branch just carries that value through. The update branch does not need to touch owner_id.

Do NOT widen any RLS policy here. The public read path uses createAdminSupabaseClient(), which bypasses RLS, and the store filters visibility explicitly - the same defense-in-depth pattern documented on getBySlug().

create table public.handle_history (
  old_handle text primary key check (old_handle ~ "^[a-z0-9_]{3,20}$"),
  profile_id uuid not null references public.profiles(id) on delete cascade,
  released_at timestamptz not null default now()
);
create index handle_history_profile_idx on public.handle_history (profile_id, released_at desc);

The primary key on old_handle is what enforces the quarantine at the schema level: while the row exists, the handle is spoken for. Reclaiming is then a matter of a claim-time check - the handle is available if it is unused in profiles AND either absent from handle_history or its released_at is older than the quarantine window (30 days).

Reclaiming by the ORIGINAL owner should be allowed immediately - the quarantine protects against impersonation by a third party, not against someone changing their mind.

RLS: publicly readable (it is part of resolving a public URL), no client writes - the profile update path writes it.

Note the interaction with profiles.handle uniqueness: releasing a handle and inserting the history row must happen in the same transaction as the profiles update, or a crash between the two leaves a handle both free and unclaimable.
